1
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:11,200
200,000 years ago, our species lives only in Africa, already in deep-rooted groups. Neanderthals (white sand) and Denisovans (pale blue) live in Eurasia.

2
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:15,600
Each glass vessel is a population, shaped like its land. Each colour of sand is a strand of ancestry.

3
00:00:16,600 --> 00:00:20,400
Separated for long enough, a group's sand drifts into a colour of its own.

4
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:27,206
Some groups venture out of Africa early, about 120,000 years ago, but leave little trace in people today.

5
00:00:30,760 --> 00:00:35,277
Sometime between 70,000 and 50,000 years ago, a small band crosses out of Africa.

6
00:00:37,431 --> 00:00:42,861
They spread fast, along the coasts to South Asia and, by boat, to New Guinea and Australia: by at least 50,000 years ago, possibly earlier.

7
00:00:43,861 --> 00:00:47,846
Along the way they mix with Neanderthals, mostly 50,000 to 43,000 years ago. People outside Africa carry about 2% Neanderthal DNA (white grains); many Africans carry a little too.

8
00:00:48,846 --> 00:00:53,458
In Island Southeast Asia and New Guinea they mix with Denisovans: Papuans and Aboriginal Australians carry 3-5% Denisovan DNA (pale blue).

9
00:00:54,458 --> 00:00:59,950
People reach Europe, East Asia and the steppe. The Neanderthals and Denisovans eventually disappear; a little of their sand lives on in us.

10
00:01:00,950 --> 00:01:05,466
Tens of thousands of years apart, each region's sand drifts into its own colour.

11
00:01:06,747 --> 00:01:10,190
In Ice Age Siberia, East Asian and Ancient North Eurasian ancestry mix.

12
00:01:11,190 --> 00:01:15,697
By at least 20,000 years ago, perhaps earlier, a few founders cross Beringia. A handful of grains seeds two whole continents: the founder effect.

13
00:01:16,697 --> 00:01:21,213
Largely separate for 15,000 years, the First Peoples of the Americas become genetically distinct.

14
00:01:23,697 --> 00:01:31,117
About 11,000 years ago farming begins in the Fertile Crescent (and later, independently, elsewhere). Farmers spread and mix with the foragers they meet.

15
00:01:38,926 --> 00:01:42,400
Herders from the steppe sweep west into Europe and later south into South Asia.

16
00:01:43,400 --> 00:01:47,000
The Bantu expansion carries West-Central African ancestry across half a continent.

17
00:01:48,200 --> 00:01:51,800
Austronesian sailors from Taiwan island-hop across two oceans...

18
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:04,387
...to Tonga and Samoa about 2,900 years ago (Hawai'i, shown here for Polynesia, about 1,000 years ago), and to Madagascar, where Asian and African ancestry meet.

19
00:02:11,606 --> 00:02:20,316
1500 CE: ships link every continent. Epidemics, war and enslavement devastate Indigenous peoples of the Americas, and 12.5 million Africans are taken across the Atlantic in chains.

20
00:02:22,891 --> 00:02:26,491
Settlers transform the makeup of Australia, Siberia and southern Africa.

21
00:02:33,292 --> 00:02:36,892
Modern migration keeps stirring the sand, and people gather in cities.

22
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:47,129
Today almost every vessel holds sand from many places. Islands of ancestry endure, from New Guinea to the Andes.

23
00:02:49,000 --> 00:03:05,400
And underneath, it was always the same sand. Every person alive descends from those first African grains.

